A formal proof, assuming the Riemann hypothesis, that at least 85.39% of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function
are distinct. Kristian Muri Knausgård, 24 November 2026.

Precisely, N_d >= (8539/10000 - o(1)) N on (T, 2T] and on (0, T]; under RH every zero is on the line, so
N0star = N_d. At each zero the explicit formula with a Fejer kernel bounds the multiplicity by a prime sum,
m(rho) <= 1/h - 2 Re W(rho) + o(1), where W(s) is the sum of Lambda(n) (1 - log n/(h log T)) n^(-s) / (h log T)
over n <= T^h, h = 0.999. An affine combination Y of W and the mollified derivative A = zeta'(rho) B(rho) / log T,
with the mollifier B of Bui and Heath-Brown for P(v) = 1.78 v - 0.78 v^2, satisfies m(rho) (1 - (Re Y(rho))^2) <= 1, so
N_d >= sum over the zeros of (1 - (Re Y(rho))^2) + o(N).  The mean of (Im Y)^2 is bounded below through its
covariance with a short prime polynomial U.  The new average is the reflected twisted first moment
sum zeta'(rho) B(rho) W(1 - rho) (on RH, W(1 - rho) is the conjugate of W(rho)), evaluated separately on the primes
up to T^theta and on the primes in (T^theta, T^h], theta = 0.4999999; a Hermitian second moment controls the thin
remaining range and all higher prime powers.  All averages over the zeros are proved here from RH and Zeta23.

